  "records": {"samples/Item.yml":"# The collection's items, as the starter shows them (docs/spec/v0.1.md, Sample data).\n- name: Stoneware teapot\n  summary: Thrown by hand on Gotland in the 1970s, with a glaze the color of wet sand. It still pours without a drip.\n  kind: favourite\n  addedOn: 2026-09-24\n- name: Bentwood chair\n  summary: One of four chairs from a café that closed in 1962. The seat was recaned last winter.\n  kind: general\n  addedOn: 2026-09-18\n- name: Brass ship's lamp\n  summary: Hung in a fishing boat out of Kökar until the boat was sold. It burns paraffin and smells of it.\n  kind: favourite\n  addedOn: 2026-09-10\n- name: Linen tablecloth\n  summary: Woven in Ostrobothnia with a pattern of small birds. Too long for any table we have.\n  kind: general\n  addedOn: 2026-09-03\n- name: Birch bark basket\n  summary: Plaited from a single strip of bark, the old way. Light enough to carry berries all day.\n  kind: general\n  link: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_bark\n  addedOn: 2026-08-27\n- name: Wall clock\n  summary: A Mora clock in a painted pine case, made around 1840. It needs winding every eight days.\n  kind: favourite\n  link: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mora_clock\n  addedOn: 2026-08-15\n- name: Enamel coffee pot\n  summary: A blue enamel pot that boiled coffee on a wood stove for forty years. Chipped at the spout, now only for show.\n  kind: archived\n  addedOn: 2026-07-30\n- name: Wool blanket\n  summary: Striped gray and red, from a mill in Jämtland that still makes them. It lives on the reading chair.\n  kind: general\n  addedOn: 2026-07-12\n"},
